{
  "result": "I love the quiet hour before the city wakes, when light comes in sideways through the blinds and makes thin gold stripes across the floor.\nThe kettle hums, the cat stretches, and my thoughts are still soft around the edges, not yet sharpened by news or notifications.\nI think about how much of life is just maintenance, watering plants, answering emails, remembering to buy milk, and how easily we mistake that for meaning.\nMaybe meaning is smaller and stranger: the way a stranger holds the door, the smell of rain on hot pavement, a song leaking from a passing car.\nYesterday on the train I watched an old man fold a newspaper with such care, as if it were a letter from someone he loved.\nNobody else noticed. Everyone was staring at glowing rectangles, scrolling through other people's breakfasts and arguments.\nI felt a sudden tenderness for all of us, trying so hard to be interesting while the most interesting thing was simply being there, breathing together in a moving metal box.\nWhen I got home, I watered my drooping basil and apologized to it out loud, which made me laugh at myself.\nThe cat looked unimpressed. The kettle had gone quiet. The gold stripes had moved higher up the wall.\nTime passes whether we notice or not, but noticing feels like a small rebellion, a way of saying I was here, I paid attention, I loved this ordinary Tuesday morning enough to remember it.\nAnd perhaps that is enough.",
